1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
is descriptive of the world.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,000
And I know I was taught this many times.

3
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,120
So for someone to say no one believes this,

4
00:00:08,120 --> 00:00:09,480
either they believe it or they were

5
00:00:09,480 --> 00:00:12,160
lying to a bunch of repressionable college students.

6
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:15,360
Both of them pretty bad.

7
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:15,860
This is me.

8
00:00:15,860 --> 00:00:16,360
Not lying.

9
00:00:16,360 --> 00:00:17,720
This is me.

10
00:00:17,720 --> 00:00:18,640
I'm not lying.

11
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:20,520
I was just using words in two different ways.

12
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:22,200
Nobody would understand the difference.

13
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:26,960
Now what is Dempset's alternative?

14
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:29,120
He has an alternative that he calls the comparative

15
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:30,040
institutions approach.

16
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:38,440
And this begins by saying nothing in the real world

17
00:00:38,440 --> 00:00:40,720
is perfectly counter-exhibition.

18
00:00:40,720 --> 00:00:41,760
It's not doable.

19
00:00:41,760 --> 00:00:44,000
There's always going to be some debit cost

20
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:45,720
no matter what you do in the real world.

21
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:47,600
So you just have to live with it.

22
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:51,080
And so instead you should be asking what fails least badly.

23
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:53,880
What fails least badly?

24
00:00:53,880 --> 00:01:00,240
And there is a parable associated with this.

25
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:00,880
No, allegory.

26
00:01:00,880 --> 00:01:02,040
So you can add allegory.

27
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:05,280
So allegory is something like George Orwell's Animal Farm

28
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:08,200
where the characters stand for other things.

29
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:09,680
So if you've ever read Animal Farm,

30
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:17,200
you know that Snowball is Trotsky and Napoleon is Stalin.

31
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:20,400
And then the Raven is supposed to be Rasputin, I've heard.

32
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:23,240
And the second time they blow up,

33
00:01:23,240 --> 00:01:25,800
the windmill, that's World War II.

34
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:26,600
All right, anyway.

35
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:29,640
So an allegory is a story where there's actually

36
00:01:29,640 --> 00:01:34,320
a mapping from the fable you're telling into the real world.

37
00:01:34,320 --> 00:01:35,760
Very close one.

38
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:38,560
So anyway, there is a fable associated

39
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:40,520
with this comparative institutions idea called

40
00:01:40,520 --> 00:01:41,880
The Tale of the Emperor.

41
00:01:41,880 --> 00:01:42,760
Or the allegory.

42
00:01:42,760 --> 00:01:44,800
The allegory goes like this.

43
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:47,680
The Emperor Rome announces a great musical contest

44
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:50,560
where he summons all the best musicians in the empire

45
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:52,600
to come to his palace and play for him.

46
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:55,240
But only two performers show up.

47
00:01:55,240 --> 00:01:57,680
The first performer plays this whole song

48
00:01:57,680 --> 00:01:59,320
and he misses one note.

49
00:01:59,320 --> 00:02:02,600
And as soon as he misses a note, the Roman Emperor says,

50
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:03,640
the contest is over.

51
00:02:03,640 --> 00:02:05,120
The second guy wins.

52
00:02:05,120 --> 00:02:08,160
He's the best singer, the best musician in the realm.

53
00:02:08,160 --> 00:02:09,920
The end.

54
00:02:09,920 --> 00:02:12,600
That's the entire allegory.

55
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:14,280
Now, what is the mapping?

56
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:15,720
Well, the Roman Emperor is supposed

57
00:02:15,720 --> 00:02:17,680
to be the economics profession.

58
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:20,120
And the first musician is supposed to be the market.

59
00:02:20,120 --> 00:02:23,200
And the second musician is supposed to be the government.

60
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:28,360
And this is especially associated with public choice.

61
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:30,560
The claim is that a lot of economists

62
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:33,480
go and look at markets, find one small problem,

63
00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:35,800
and then say, look, government is the solution here,

64
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:37,400
without ever actually, empirically

65
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:39,000
studying government.

66
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,120
That's what corresponds to giving them

67
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:44,200
the prize for ever listening to their music.

68
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:46,080
And of course, the whole idea of public choice

69
00:02:46,080 --> 00:02:48,560
is we are going to empirically study government.

70
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:50,160
And then when we empirically study it,

71
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:52,160
we may find that it's even worse.

72
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:53,560
It might miss two notes.

73
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:55,960
It might miss all the notes.

74
00:02:55,960 --> 00:03:00,200
So we'll come back to that in the back as the tale of the emperor.

75
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:02,080
All right, so questions about that?

76
00:03:04,840 --> 00:03:09,760
So in that case, last concept, the concept of the second best.

77
00:03:09,760 --> 00:03:13,400
So when you add more constraints to a standard problem,

78
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:15,960
it often turns out the original option was no longer feasible.

79
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:19,440
So think about how you drove to school tonight.

80
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:21,720
You were already optimizing a bunch of things, right?

81
00:03:21,720 --> 00:03:23,400
Now, I suppose I add on the constraints.

82
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:26,520
You also have to pick up some Kentucky fried chicken for me.

83
00:03:26,520 --> 00:03:28,840
All right, now, does anyone actually drive exactly

84
00:03:28,840 --> 00:03:31,440
past a KFC on their way here?

85
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:32,600
Pop-up.

86
00:03:32,600 --> 00:03:33,480
What's that?

87
00:03:33,480 --> 00:03:35,160
You're not passing the pop-up.

88
00:03:35,160 --> 00:03:36,080
No, no, no, no, no.

89
00:03:36,080 --> 00:03:36,580
Not good.

90
00:03:36,580 --> 00:03:38,720
It's got to be KFC.

91
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:40,440
All right, so as a result of this,

92
00:03:40,440 --> 00:03:42,160
if I impose this as a constraint on you,

93
00:03:42,160 --> 00:03:45,920
what will this automatically do to your commuting time?

94
00:03:45,920 --> 00:03:47,340
It will raise your commuting time

95
00:03:47,340 --> 00:03:50,400
when I add an extra constraint, unless it just so likely

96
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:54,280
happens that my constraint corresponds

97
00:03:54,280 --> 00:03:55,520
to what you were doing anyway.

98
00:03:55,520 --> 00:03:58,000
But of course, that would almost never happen.

99
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:00,720
So that is a general idea in optimization.

100
00:04:00,720 --> 00:04:02,180
Whenever you add a new constraint,

101
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:05,840
you should expect that your last optimal will no longer

102
00:04:05,840 --> 00:04:08,420
be feasible, and you're going to have to do something that is

103
00:04:08,420 --> 00:04:12,320
harder or takes more time or is more painful.

104
00:04:12,320 --> 00:04:15,960
And economists will often refer to the original optimum

105
00:04:15,960 --> 00:04:19,680
as a first-best solution.

106
00:04:19,680 --> 00:04:21,800
So the original optimum before you

107
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:23,880
add the extra constraints first-best,

108
00:04:23,880 --> 00:04:28,720
and then the second one, the thing

109
00:04:28,720 --> 00:04:31,720
that is optimal to do after one more constraint has been piled

110
00:04:31,720 --> 00:04:35,120
on your to-do list, a second-best.

111
00:04:35,120 --> 00:04:36,960
So an example.

112
00:04:36,960 --> 00:04:39,600
Economists often think a price equals marginal cost

113
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:41,840
is first-best, until you say, yeah, well,

114
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:43,680
there's going to be a constraint that firms

115
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:46,240
can't lose money forever.

116
00:04:46,240 --> 00:04:47,680
Can't lose money forever.

117
00:04:47,680 --> 00:04:49,720
In that case, a second-best optimum

118
00:04:49,720 --> 00:04:52,480
might be price equals to average cost,

119
00:04:52,480 --> 00:04:55,840
even in an industry where you've got declining costs.

120
00:04:55,840 --> 00:04:59,200
Because given the constraint that firms

121
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:01,840
can't lose money forever, what are they supposed to do?

122
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:02,520
Accelerate them.

123
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:04,400
You can't go and make them price a marginal cost,

124
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:06,140
because they'll be driven out of business,

125
00:05:06,140 --> 00:05:08,200
and far worse to have zero firms that

126
00:05:08,200 --> 00:05:12,160
have monopoly charging more than marginal cost, right?

127
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:14,560
So there's that.

128
00:05:14,560 --> 00:05:15,080
All right.

129
00:05:15,080 --> 00:05:16,560
And then occasionally, you'll hear economists

130
00:05:16,560 --> 00:05:18,800
say really weird things like, this is not first-best

131
00:05:18,800 --> 00:05:19,440
or second-best.

132
00:05:19,440 --> 00:05:21,000
It's more like 15th-best.

133
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,040
And this is when they are talking about a very thorny

134
00:05:24,040 --> 00:05:26,240
real-world problem, where there are

135
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:29,000
so many different constraints that they have to deal with.

136
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:30,960
And of course, normally, it isn't literally

137
00:05:30,960 --> 00:05:32,560
14 extra constraints.

138
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:34,560
Normally, it's just, look, so hard.

139
00:05:34,560 --> 00:05:37,600
There's so many different people causing problems,

140
00:05:37,600 --> 00:05:39,960
so many different complex issues.

141
00:05:39,960 --> 00:05:42,960
Someone's talking about privatizing telecom.

142
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:45,880
Look, we've got to deal with our universal access requirement.

143
00:05:45,880 --> 00:05:51,040
We've got to deal with the minority business requirement.

144
00:05:51,040 --> 00:05:54,400
We've got to deal with a bundling issue.

145
00:05:54,400 --> 00:05:56,680
So, oh my god, it's so terrible.

146
00:05:56,680 --> 00:05:59,400
So anyway, if you've ever heard economists talk in these terms,

147
00:05:59,400 --> 00:06:02,080
that's what they're talking about.

148
00:06:02,080 --> 00:06:02,580
Questions?

149
00:06:02,580 --> 00:06:04,960
Anyone happy with that?

150
00:06:04,960 --> 00:06:08,280
Everyone's so tired that you no longer feel happy or unhappy

151
00:06:08,280 --> 00:06:10,760
about economics.

152
00:06:10,760 --> 00:06:11,920
Of course not.

153
00:06:11,920 --> 00:06:13,600
You will never be in the position

154
00:06:13,600 --> 00:06:16,280
where you cannot feel happy or unhappy about economics.

155
00:06:16,280 --> 00:06:17,680
It's always a pure joy.

156
00:06:20,240 --> 00:06:22,680
And then moving on, so last topic

157
00:06:22,680 --> 00:06:24,040
in economics and philosophy we're

158
00:06:24,040 --> 00:06:25,840
going to spend much time on.

159
00:06:25,840 --> 00:06:27,960
So moral philosophy and efficiency.

160
00:06:27,960 --> 00:06:30,760
So who cares about efficiency anyway?

161
00:06:30,760 --> 00:06:33,040
What difference does it possibly make?

162
00:06:33,040 --> 00:06:35,160
So is there anyone on earth who seriously believes

163
00:06:35,160 --> 00:06:38,960
that the right action is always the counter-insufficient one?

164
00:06:38,960 --> 00:06:41,040
The answer is yes, there are at least a few people

165
00:06:41,040 --> 00:06:43,040
who have endorsed this view.

166
00:06:43,040 --> 00:06:46,680
This Harvard law and economics guy, Steve Chabell,

167
00:06:46,680 --> 00:06:48,920
I was not there, but I heard from a liable source

168
00:06:48,920 --> 00:06:51,760
that he referred to people who care about anything

169
00:06:51,760 --> 00:06:55,040
other than counter-insufficiency as the dark side.

170
00:06:55,040 --> 00:06:57,440
The whole talk was, those on the dark side

171
00:06:57,440 --> 00:07:00,080
believe that things other than efficiency matter.

172
00:07:00,080 --> 00:07:01,720
But think about what that would mean.

173
00:07:01,720 --> 00:07:03,960
It means sometimes we'd have to do inefficient things.

174
00:07:03,960 --> 00:07:05,880
And that's really inefficient.

175
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:07,400
So no.

176
00:07:07,400 --> 00:07:10,520
All right, so maybe you've spent better than that.

177
00:07:10,520 --> 00:07:12,800
And if you go and read early Richard Posner, not

178
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:14,920
modern Posner, who has a very confusing view,

179
00:07:14,920 --> 00:07:17,520
but early Posner, does sound like he just

180
00:07:17,520 --> 00:07:19,720
thinks that whatever is counter-insufficient is exactly

181
00:07:19,720 --> 00:07:21,600
what you ought to do.

182
00:07:21,600 --> 00:07:23,360
So there are some people like this.

183
00:07:23,360 --> 00:07:26,240
And Robin Hanson occasionally talks this way, too,

184
00:07:26,240 --> 00:07:28,680
although his real view is a bit more subtle than that.

185
00:07:28,680 --> 00:07:33,480
In particular, he doesn't have to do what's counter-insufficient.

186
00:07:33,480 --> 00:07:36,280
But in some way, social rules should still

187
00:07:36,280 --> 00:07:37,280
be counter-insufficient.

188
00:07:37,280 --> 00:07:39,360
And my question is, well, if no individual

189
00:07:39,360 --> 00:07:41,600
is obligated to do the counter-insufficient thing,

190
00:07:41,600 --> 00:07:45,160
then how can society be obligated to do it?

191
00:07:45,160 --> 00:07:47,080
And I think his answer is, no, you're

192
00:07:47,080 --> 00:07:52,280
moralizing a claim that I don't need as a moral claim.

193
00:07:52,280 --> 00:07:53,920
But then what is it exactly?

194
00:07:53,920 --> 00:07:55,880
So again, Robin actually calls his view

195
00:07:55,880 --> 00:07:58,840
dealism and is sort of the view that we should do it.

196
00:07:58,840 --> 00:08:00,600
Like, counter-insufficiency is a good way

197
00:08:00,600 --> 00:08:05,280
of ballparking the deals that we would make if we all

198
00:08:05,280 --> 00:08:07,760
had a big social bargain.

199
00:08:07,760 --> 00:08:12,000
But I will say his view on a lot of times

200
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:13,800
I can channel Robin very actively.

201
00:08:13,800 --> 00:08:16,000
This is one where I still find his view so confusing

202
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:19,800
that I can't pull it off.

203
00:08:19,800 --> 00:08:21,960
Now, a popular view of many many economists

204
00:08:21,960 --> 00:08:24,120
is to say, sure, counter-insufficiency

205
00:08:24,120 --> 00:08:26,800
is crazy because why?

206
00:08:26,800 --> 00:08:28,200
One problem.

207
00:08:28,200 --> 00:08:30,640
Say, counter-insufficiency is a perfect standard

208
00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:33,120
except for one problem, which is just

209
00:08:33,120 --> 00:08:35,680
that it fails to account for diminishing

210
00:08:35,680 --> 00:08:37,480
marginal utility of wealth.

211
00:08:37,480 --> 00:08:40,040
So it puts way too much weight on the rich, way too

212
00:08:40,040 --> 00:08:41,320
little weight on the poor.

213
00:08:41,320 --> 00:08:43,440
But all we need to do is make a wealth adjustment,

214
00:08:43,440 --> 00:08:44,940
and then we should always do whatever

215
00:08:44,940 --> 00:08:47,480
is counter-insufficient.

216
00:08:47,480 --> 00:08:48,600
So that is a view.

217
00:08:48,600 --> 00:08:49,320
It's out there.

218
00:08:49,320 --> 00:08:50,320
So you should be aware.

219
00:08:50,320 --> 00:08:52,840
There's a bunch of smart people who think that.

220
00:08:52,840 --> 00:08:55,320
I mean, to me, this is like, well, look,

221
00:08:55,320 --> 00:08:58,880
if one exception is OK, why not a whole bunch of exceptions?

222
00:08:58,880 --> 00:09:02,320
So one that bothers me, how about dessert?

223
00:09:02,320 --> 00:09:06,000
Sometimes someone actually created the value.

224
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:09,120
And don't they just deserve it, even if they don't eat it?

225
00:09:09,120 --> 00:09:12,320
And there's other people just sat by being lazy bums,

226
00:09:12,320 --> 00:09:13,920
being the grasshopper in the fable.

227
00:09:13,920 --> 00:09:15,960
But shouldn't the grasshopper really get nothing

228
00:09:15,960 --> 00:09:17,640
and the ant get what he worked for,

229
00:09:17,640 --> 00:09:20,320
and the grasshopper back up and improve and shape up,

230
00:09:20,320 --> 00:09:23,120
and the ant get to sit and enjoy the fruits of his labor?

231
00:09:23,120 --> 00:09:25,640
Wouldn't that be the fair thing?

232
00:09:25,640 --> 00:09:28,760
So in which case, you might say, well, actually,

233
00:09:28,760 --> 00:09:31,800
like your adjustment for diminishing marginality wealth

234
00:09:31,800 --> 00:09:33,320
gives the wrong answer.

235
00:09:33,320 --> 00:09:35,680
Now, this seems like a crazy answer to you.

236
00:09:35,680 --> 00:09:39,240
Here's a case where it might not seem so crazy.

237
00:09:39,240 --> 00:09:40,960
Suppose you're a professor.

238
00:09:40,960 --> 00:09:45,120
Are you going to give out grades based upon need or dessert?

239
00:09:45,120 --> 00:09:48,360
So who should get the A's, the students who need A's the most

240
00:09:48,360 --> 00:09:50,240
or the students who did well in the class

241
00:09:50,240 --> 00:09:52,160
and did the work and understand the material?

242
00:09:54,800 --> 00:09:56,880
What are you going to do when you're a professor,

243
00:09:56,880 --> 00:09:58,560
if that's what you want to do?

244
00:09:58,560 --> 00:10:00,360
A lot of you will be professors in the end.

245
00:10:00,360 --> 00:10:02,960
Are you going to hand out grades based upon need?

246
00:10:02,960 --> 00:10:03,460
I don't.

247
00:10:03,460 --> 00:10:05,080
You hand out everybody's name on a dart

248
00:10:05,080 --> 00:10:06,400
and just throw it at the board?

249
00:10:06,400 --> 00:10:07,320
Yeah, you can do that.

250
00:10:07,320 --> 00:10:09,480
That's another moral theory.

251
00:10:09,480 --> 00:10:12,000
One side of the student just said, look, you have to give me

252
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:13,680
an A instead of a C, because a C is

253
00:10:13,680 --> 00:10:15,680
unacceptable to my conservative Indian parents.

254
00:10:15,680 --> 00:10:19,440
Well, but giving out free grades is unacceptable to me.

255
00:10:19,440 --> 00:10:19,940
Sorry.

256
00:10:19,940 --> 00:10:20,960
So no.

257
00:10:20,960 --> 00:10:22,560
If they are the ones who love you,

258
00:10:22,560 --> 00:10:24,880
you're going to have to talk to them and explain to them,

259
00:10:24,880 --> 00:10:27,720
I'm not giving you a free grade.

260
00:10:27,720 --> 00:10:28,220
Right, NFG?

261
00:10:28,220 --> 00:10:30,760
You say, look, be utilitarian, man.

262
00:10:30,760 --> 00:10:33,160
Look, no one's ever even going to know that you did this.

263
00:10:33,160 --> 00:10:34,000
I'll be better off.

264
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:35,160
My parents will be happier.

265
00:10:35,160 --> 00:10:36,120
No one will ever know.

